  "name": "Positive carriers",
  "description": "Two strips of different metals in your solenoid's field, each with two floating contacts on its sides that read its Hall voltage. The upper one conducts by electrons; the lower one's carriers are positive (orange). Some real metals, such as zinc and beryllium, behave so: band theory explains it, and in this classical model their carriers simply carry a positive charge. Make a current between 2.7 and 3.5 flow into the upper strip's right end, and make both strips' top contacts sit above their bottom ones: the upper by 1.6 to 2.8, the lower by 0.6 to 1.6 (averaged over $t = 10$ to $80$). Which way must each current flow?",
